61,3,1,talk,Toshio,,"Option D is the strongest candidate. It provides broad, cross-departmental utility and avoids the hazardous ground-floor risks of Option B and the external weather delays associated with Option C.","{""words"":29,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,Victoria,,"Option B addresses the critical shortage of general-purpose rooms. While Option D is useful, the demand for study and group space is constant, and B avoids the high mechanical costs associated with Option A.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,George,,"Option A is the only choice that addresses the technicians' burden. Replacing the extraction equipment would eliminate those tedious manual routines, providing essential relief to a staff that has been stretched too thin for far too long.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Mizuki,,"Option A and C are far too risky. A involves complex mechanical plant upgrades, and C is entirely exposed to weather. Both are recipes for cost overruns and scheduling slips. I favor Option B.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,1,talk,Benjamin,,"Option A is a financial fantasy given the surge in mechanical costs, and Option D ignores our lack of AV renewal reserves. Option B is the only rational choice that avoids these volatile expenses.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Toshio,,"Option B is risky due to the hazardous materials found in ground-floor partitions. Option D serves the widest user base and avoids those structural dangers, making it the most reliable choice for overall satisfaction.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,Victoria,,"While Option D is useful, it is a specialist facility. My data shows specialist rooms often sit idle, whereas general-purpose space is in constant demand. Option B solves a real, daily operational crisis for our students.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,George,,"I understand the risks, but we cannot keep ignoring the people. Option A is not just about equipment; it is about the health and sanity of our technicians. We must prioritize their relief over study space.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Mizuki,,"Option A's mechanical complexity and Option C's weather exposure make them non-starters. Between B and D, B's internal nature is safer for the schedule, despite Toshio's concerns about the ground floor.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,2,talk,Benjamin,,"It is charming that we consider Option D, yet we have no fund to maintain its electronics. Option B may have its quirks, but it avoids the market's current appetite for mechanical inflation.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,Toshio,,"If we prioritize long-term success, we must avoid the ground-floor hazards of Option B. Option D provides the cross-departmental utility that historically yields the highest satisfaction, without the structural risks that cause months of delay.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,Victoria,,"The demand for general-purpose space is an urgent, daily reality. Option B addresses this directly. While Option D is versatile, it remains a specialist facility, and we cannot ignore the constant shortage of general study areas.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,3,talk,George,,"We are discussing space and risks, but we are forgetting the people who keep this university running. Option A is the only plan that provides tangible relief to our exhausted technicians. I cannot support any other choice.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,Mizuki,,"Option A is a scheduling nightmare waiting to happen. Option C is out due to weather risks. Between B and D, B is the most stable choice for our timeline and budget.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,3,talk,Benjamin,,It is a curious exercise in optimism to suggest Option D when we have no reserve for its inevitable obsolescence. Option B is the only choice that doesn't treat our budget as a work of fiction.,"{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
76,3,4,talk,Toshio,,"Despite the risks, Option B seems to have the most support. However, I remain convinced that Option D offers the best balance of utility and safety. I will vote for the most sustainable outcome.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
77,3,4,talk,Victoria,,The evidence is clear: general-purpose space is our most acute shortage. Option B directly solves this operational crisis and avoids the high mechanical costs of Option A. I will vote for Option B.,"{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
78,3,4,talk,George,,I cannot in good conscience vote for a plan that ignores our staff. The technicians are at a breaking point. I will vote for Option A to ensure they finally get the relief they need.,"{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
79,3,4,talk,Mizuki,,I cannot support A or C due to the high probability of delays. Option B is the most realistic choice for our schedule and budget. I will vote for Option B.,"{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
80,3,4,talk,Benjamin,,Logic dictates that we avoid the mechanical inflation of Option A and the maintenance void of Option D. Option B is the only choice that remains grounded in financial reality. I vote for Option B.,"{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
